1:---
2:name: adversarial-read-only-review
3:description: Review a scoped implementation or commit without mutations when the user says "Read-only" or requests an auditable German technical verdict.
4:argument-hint: "[repo or evidence scope]"
5:user-invocable: false
6:allowed-tools: Read, Grep, Glob, Bash
7:---
8:
9:# Adversarial read-only review
10:
11:## When to use
12:
13:Use for implementation, commit, diff, bundle, or plan reviews explicitly constrained as read-only, especially when the requested result is a German verdict with findings. Do not use for implementation, remediation, or a broad security audit without a defined evidence scope.
14:
15:## Inputs / context to gather
16:
17:1. Record the exact review boundary: named files/bundles, commit/base, briefs, rulings, plan, and requested output format.
18:2. Read the binding contracts before implementation reports or reported test totals.
19:3. Identify whether write-free probes are permitted. Treat all supplied test results as Fremdevidenz unless independently run.
20:
21:## Procedure
22:
23:1. Do not edit, install, commit, stage, run mutating tests, or change Git configuration. For Python expression probes, use `-B` or `PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1`.
24:2. Inspect the complete scoped diff and relevant implementation/tests. For each claimed omission, check code, comments, docs, and the binding plan separately.
25:3. Trace security and state boundaries: canonical/Windows Junction paths, provenance, secrets in parser diagnostics, blocklist views, lock/race windows, rollback/recovery, stale state, deletion/rename behavior, and foreign Git staging.
26:4. Use small in-memory or explicitly write-free probes only to demonstrate a suspected contract violation. Record observed output, not assumptions.
27:5. Separate confirmed positives, own verification, and Fremdevidenz. Do not promote green tests to proof when their counterproperties were not tested.
28:6. Write the requested German report: one-sentence verdict first; then `Spec-Treue` and `Qualitaet`; then numbered `T<n>`/requested findings with severity, classification, Datei:Zeile, problem, evidence, and concrete fix. State scope boundaries and justified omissions.
29:
30:## Efficiency plan
31:
32:- Start from the binding plan/rulings and diff, then grep contracts into the changed code; avoid broad checkout exploration outside the agreed scope.
33:- Focus probes on claimed safety properties and race/error paths. Stop probing once a reproducible, contract-relevant failure is established and preserve the exact concise observation.
34:- Reuse a single safe-directory option per Git call when necessary; never set it globally.
35:
36:## Pitfalls and fixes
37:
38:- Reported green tests -> they may miss adversarial paths -> label as Fremdevidenz and construct a focused counterprobe when permitted.
39:- `python` import creates bytecode -> use `-B` before importing.
40:- Lexical path checks -> Junction/reparse escapes remain -> validate every canonical segment and every temporary/protocol path before mutation.
41:- One-time pre-lock or pre-commit checks -> concurrent status/index/ref changes bypass them -> reload/revalidate under the same lock or use an isolated atomic transaction.
42:- Raw parser errors -> secrets/config data leak -> normalize all input-driven parse and constructor failures to content-free diagnostics.
43:
44:## Verification checklist
45:
46:- Review stayed within its evidence and mutation boundary.
47:- Every requested omission reason was independently assessed.
